finding.evidence:
`styleOffAsClear` keeps an explicit `false` only when the block's *named style* supplies the key (`if (defaults[key]) continue;`, document.ts:413). `underline` is in BOOLEAN_INLINE_STYLE_KEYS (document.ts:42), and no built-in/override named style supplies it, so a Cmd+U / toolbar underline-off is now rewritten to `underline: undefined` (key deleted by `mergeInlineStyle`, block-helpers.ts:322).

But link runs have a *second* implicit default layer that the guard does not consult — packages/docs/src/view/paint-layout.ts:384-391:
```
let showUnderline = style.underline ?? false;
if (style.href) {
  if (style.underline === undefined) showUnderline = true;   // line 390
}
```
The comment there says "user-set values take precedence" — the stored `false` was exactly what suppressed the link underline. `insertLink` writes only `{ href: url }` (editor.ts:2962), never `underline: true`, so a link's underline exists solely through that `undefined` branch.

Consequence: on a link run, pressing underline-off now clears the key and the painter re-underlines it. Worse, the read side (`visitStyledRunsInRange`, range-runs.ts:64) merges only named-style defaults, so the summary reports underline OFF for a run that paints underlined — the next press writes `underline: true` (visually identical) and the one after clears again. Underline can never be removed from a link, where before the change it could. No test covers underline-off on an href run (searched packages/docs/test for `underline` — only docx-map, clipboard, paint-layout composing, and the block-helpers apply tests), so CI will not catch it.
